{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"Los Angeles","level":"city","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"LA Rent Stabilization Ordinance: no-fault eviction relocation assistance","requirement":"Before a no-fault eviction from an RSO unit, the landlord must file a Declaration of Intent to Evict with LAHD and pay relocation assistance. Termination notices may be served only after the declaration is filed, and a copy of the notice must be filed with LAHD within 3 days of service. Payment must be made available within 15 days of service of the written notice, or placed in escrow within that period. Payment is per unit, not per tenant, and is split pro rata among tenants. If more than one amount applies, the higher amount is paid. Not-at-fault reasons: owner or relative or resident manager occupancy, demolition, conversion or removal from the rental market (Ellis Act), a governmental order to vacate, HUD recovery of possession before sale, residential hotel unit conversion or demolition, and conversion to affordable housing. A reduced amount applies to qualifying Mom and Pop owner or family occupancy evictions. Landlords are exempt when replacing one resident manager with another, or when a natural disaster creates hazardous conditions through no negligence of the landlord. Accumulated rent may be offset against relocation assistance, except for government orders to vacate.","key_value":"Effective 2026-07-01 to 2027-06-30: eligible tenants $11,000 (under 3 years) or $14,400 (3+ years or low income); qualified tenants $23,150 or $27,400; Mom and Pop properties $10,550 (eligible) or $21,250 (qualified). Amounts increase every July 1.","coverage_conditions":"Units covered by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance. Qualified tenants are age 62+, handicapped or disabled, or have a minor dependent child. Mom and Pop reduction requires a building of 4 or fewer units, no use of the provision in the prior 3 years, the landlord owning no more than 4 units plus one single-family home on a separate lot in LA, and any relative not owning residential property in LA.","exemptions":"Replacing a resident manager with another resident manager; hazardous conditions from a natural disaster not caused by landlord negligence. Demolition for new construction of Protected Units with lower-income tenants is governed by the RPO and HCA instead.","coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":"For demolition of Protected Units for new construction, the higher of State HCA or City RPO/RSO amounts applies.","yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":"Failure to file the Landlord Declaration makes the eviction a violation of the RSO or JCO; failure to pay relocation may be raised as an affirmative defense to eviction.","citation":"L.A.M.C. § 151.09","quoted_span":"Failure to file the Landlord Declaration with the LAHD makes the eviction a violation of the RSO or JCO.","supporting_spans":["Payment shall be made available within fifteen (15) days of service of the written notice of eviction; however,"],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"Los Angeles","level":"city","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"LA Just Cause Ordinance: no-fault eviction relocation assistance","requirement":"Before a no-fault eviction from a JCO unit, the landlord must file a Declaration of Intent to Evict with LAHD and pay relocation assistance within 15 days of service of the notice. A copy of the termination notice must be filed with LAHD within 3 days. Not-at-fault reasons are listed in LAMC 165.03 (owner, relative or manager occupancy; demolition, conversion or removal from the rental market; government order to vacate; HUD recovery of possession; residential hotel conversion; affordable housing conversion). A single-family dwelling owned by a natural person who owns no more than four dwelling units plus one single-family home on a separate lot in LA pays one month's rent, as payment or credit.","key_value":"Effective 2026-07-01 to 2027-06-30: eligible tenants $11,000 (under 3 years) or $14,400 (3+ years or low income); qualified tenants $23,150 or $27,400; natural-person single-family owners pay one month's rent.","coverage_conditions":"Residential units covered by the Just Cause Ordinance.","exemptions":"Resident manager replacement; natural disaster hazards; relocation offset for accumulated rent (except government orders to vacate).","coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":"Failure to file the Landlord Declaration makes the eviction a violation of the JCO.","citation":"L.A.M.C. § 165.03","quoted_span":"Relocation Assistance is paid per unit, not per tenant.","supporting_spans":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"Los Angeles","level":"city","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"LA Resident Protections Ordinance: demolition for new construction","requirement":"For Protected Units demolished for new construction within 5 years after the filing of an Intent to Withdraw or Declaration of Intent to Evict, owners must comply with RPO relocation rules by choosing one of three options. Owners must pay the highest of the applicable State and City amounts. Occupant protections include the right to relocation, the right to remain up to 6 months before construction (with a 6-month notice to move out), the right to return if demolition does not proceed, the right of first refusal for some tenants, and tenant notification requirements.","key_value":"Option 1 standardized payments to lower-income households (effective 2026-07-01 to 2027-06-30): extremely low $115,480, very low $96,750, low $87,450. Households above low income receive RSO/JCO amounts.","coverage_conditions":"Applies only to Protected Units (including all RSO units) demolished for new construction.","exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":"Works alongside the State Housing Crisis Act; the owner pays the higher of the State or City amounts.","yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":"2025-02-11","effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":"2025-02-11","end_date":null,"penalty":"Financial penalties starting at $250,000 per displaced unit and withholding of LAHD permit clearances.","citation":"L.A. Ordinance Nos. 188481 and 188482","quoted_span":"The RPO only applies to Protected Units that will be demolished for the purpose of new construction.","supporting_spans":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"CA","level":"state","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"Housing Crisis Act: relocation for lower-income tenants displaced by demolition","requirement":"Lower-income households (80% AMI or lower) displaced by demolition of Protected Units where the owner will build new construction must receive relocation benefits consistent with Gov. Code Chapter 16 (§ 7260 et seq.). Landlords must pay the higher of State HCA or City amounts. The HCA was introduced by SB 330 and updated by SB 8 and AB 1218.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Lower-income households displaced by demolition of Protected Units for new construction.","exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":"The owner must pay the higher of State and City relocation amounts.","yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"Cal. Gov. Code § 66300 et seq.","quoted_span":"Pay relocation assistance to tenants in compliance with the State law’s Housing Crisis Act","supporting_spans":[],"confidence":0.6,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"Los Angeles","level":"city","category":"rent_increase_limits","status":"in_force","title":"LA Rent Stabilization Ordinance (existence only)","requirement":"The document only refers to units covered by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance; it states no rent increase cap, coverage cutoffs or exemptions.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":null,"exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO)","quoted_span":"Failure to file the Landlord Declaration with the LAHD makes the eviction a violation of the RSO or JCO.","supporting_spans":[],"confidence":0.3,"conflict_note":null}],"notes":"The document is LAHD relocation assistance bulletins A and B. No security deposit, fee, screening or algorithmic pricing rules appear, and no rent increase or coverage cutoffs are stated. The first Chart A is labelled effective 2026-07-01 to 2027-06-30, but the fee chart in bulletin B is labelled 2025-07-01 to 2026-06-30, which I did not resolve. The bulletins do not give effective dates for the RSO or JCO provisions."}
